\section{Introduction}

When attention layers are added to three different recurrent architectures, their LAMBADA performance becomes near-identical: cross-architecture standard deviation drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. Yet this aggregate convergence hides an asymmetry. The three architectures reach similar overall accuracy by helping and hurting almost entirely different examples, with pairwise Jaccard similarity below 0.10. This tension between aggregate agreement and per-example disagreement arises in the context of a rapid architectural shift. Hybrid recurrent-attention models, which interleave attention layers within linear recurrent backbones, have become a dominant design for efficient language models. Jamba \citep{lieber2024jamba} combines Mamba layers with Transformer blocks; Griffin \citep{de2024griffin} mixes gated linear recurrences with local attention; RWKV-6 \citep{peng2024eagle} integrates matrix-valued states with attention-like mechanisms; RecurrentGemma \citep{botev2024recurrentgemma} pairs linear recurrences with sliding-window attention. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide a controlled model zoo spanning three recurrent architectures, five attention ratios, and two parameter scales, which enables systematic study of how attention allocation affects these hybrid designs.

Most evaluations of hybrid architectures compare aggregate benchmark scores and report that a given attention ratio achieves a certain accuracy averaged across tasks. This practice can mask substantial per-example behavioral diversity. \citet{damour2022underspecification} showed that models with equivalent held-out performance can disagree on individual predictions, a phenomenon they termed underspecification. \citet{mccoy2020berts} demonstrated that BERT models trained from different random seeds produce similar aggregate scores but diverge on specific linguistic constructions. Whether this gap between aggregate and per-example behavior extends to hybrid recurrent architectures, where the source of variation is architectural rather than random initialization, remains unquantified. The question matters because practitioners increasingly choose between recurrent backbones based on aggregate benchmarks; if architectures that score identically in aggregate behave differently on individual inputs, such choices carry hidden consequences for deployment reliability.

We present the first systematic per-example analysis of how attention affects cross-architecture agreement in hybrid recurrent models. Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, we compare three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios (0\% to 25\%) and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). We evaluate all 30 configurations on 10 benchmarks and conduct a 5{,}153-example decomposition on LAMBADA, where each example is classified as helped, hurt, or unchanged when attention is added to each architecture. Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling} previews our central result: aggregate convergence and per-example divergence follow different trajectories as attention ratio increases.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_decoupling_hero.pdf}
\caption{Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled. (a) Cross-architecture standard deviation on LAMBADA decreases gradually with attention ratio at 1.3B (solid) but partially rebounds at 340M (dashed). (b) Pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped/hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat.}
\label{fig:decoupling}
\end{figure}

Our analysis yields three findings. First, attention-driven convergence is task-specific. Of 10 benchmarks, only LAMBADA exhibits monotonic reduction in cross-architecture variance ($\rho = -1.0$, BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$; cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops 79\%). Benchmarks that involve reasoning or comprehension show no convergence, with all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$ and $p \geq .233$. The Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict correction at $\alpha = 0.05$; we treat the confidence interval as primary evidence. Second, convergence depends on scale. At 1.3B parameters the $\sigma$ trajectory is monotonically decreasing, but at 340M it is non-monotonic: $\sigma$ partially rebounds at the highest attention ratio, with only 36\% net reduction versus 79\% at 1.3B. Third, and central to our title-level claim, aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled. Cross-architecture $\sigma$ on LAMBADA decreases gradually across attention ratios ($0.024 \to 0.016 \to 0.014 \to 0.006 \to 0.005$), but pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped and hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat at approximately 0.095. Net asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles increases from 0.013 to 0.040 as attention rises. These two quantities operate on different timescales: aggregate performance equalizes progressively, while per-example specificity is fixed from the earliest attention allocation.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We quantify task-specific convergence: only retrieval tasks (LAMBADA) show attention-driven cross-architecture convergence at 1.3B scale; reasoning and comprehension benchmarks do not (\S\ref{ssec:task_convergence}, Table~\ref{tab:convergence}, Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}).
    \item We characterize scale-dependent convergence: the monotonic pattern at 1.3B becomes non-monotonic at 340M, which suggests that sufficient scale is a necessary condition for complete equalization (\S\ref{ssec:scale_convergence}).
    \item We demonstrate that aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled: attention reduces cross-architecture variance gradually, but per-example overlap saturates at the first attention level and does not increase further, while inter-architecture asymmetry grows (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}, Table~\ref{tab:per_example}, Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).
\end{itemize}

